#!/bin/tdsh
# tiny89.tdsh - load the Tiny89 core and boot the TI-89 Titanium on the AE350.
#
# Run it from the desktop -- right-click its icon and choose Run -- or from the
# Terminal or the console prompt:
#
#     tdsh run /root/Desktop/tiny89.tdsh
#
# Put it on the card with TinyTang's tool, from this repository:
#
#     python3 "$TINYTANG_DIR/tools/tinytang_put.py" --port /dev/ttyACM0 \
#         scripts/tiny89.tdsh /root/Desktop/tiny89.tdsh
#
# It needs three files on the card and checks each before it starts:
#
#     the core     /root/cores/console138k/tiny89.bin      tangload programs the FPGA with it
#     the program  /root/ae350/tiemu_smoke.elf             ae350run sends it to the AE350
#     the upgrade  /root/media/bios/TI89Titanium_OS.89u    the BIOS, sent when the program asks
#
# Each path may be overridden by setting the variable in the session first;
# `tdsh run` passes no arguments, but the script inherits the session's
# variables:
#
#     BIOS=/root/media/bios/other.89u; tdsh run /root/Desktop/tiny89.tdsh
#
# What the user provides is TI's own upgrade file (`**TIFL**`, 1,400,021 bytes),
# not a raw image: the program asks for it over the link and the converter
# inside the program builds the 4 MiB flash image on the board. The operating
# system is proprietary and lives outside this repository.
#
# The order is the one that works. tangload puts the core in, and ae350run then
# loads the program, serves it the upgrade, and stays with it: once the program
# prints `TinyNES LINK` -- which it does when the BIOS has been converted and
# the emulator is running -- ae350run hands the link back to the desk layer, so
# the Bluetooth keyboard drives the calculator from that moment on and F12
# still swaps between it and TinyDesk.
#
# There is deliberately no `fpga` probe between the two. A freshly loaded core
# sits in its boot ROM's loader mode, and the desk link only sees TinyTang's
# frames while the UART's link mode is set -- which the program sets, not the
# boot ROM (fpga/tiny89_soc/exts_uart.sv routes received bytes to the desk link
# only when CTRL bit 0 is set, and firmware/apps/tiemu_smoke/main.c:318 is the
# only thing that sets it). So `fpga` cannot be answered at this point on this
# core and such a check can never pass; measured on this board on 2026-10-16,
# where `fpga` answers `core 84` for the desktop core, whose own program is
# already running, and does not answer at all for a core just tangloaded.
# ae350run makes the check that matters instead: it waits for the boot ROM's
# own `TinyNES LOAD` and reports when that does not come.
#
# Understand the failure mode before relying on the checks above. If this script
# stops before the program runs, the board is left in loader mode: the core's
# video was never enabled, so the screen shows the fabric's colour bars, and
# both the desk layer and the keyboard need link mode, so no key reaches
# TinyDesk and F12 cannot bring it back. That state cannot be left from the
# keyboard. A power cycle is the way out and is safe -- nothing here reflashes
# the FPGA, so the card's own core returns.
#
# Saving is Ctrl+S, which is the program's own key rather than the calculator's
# and works whatever the calculator is doing. Three things are worth knowing
# before relying on it. The board says on the Terminal what became of a save;
# only `saved <n> bytes ... read back and verified <crc>` means the card holds
# it, and anything else means it does not. A save that does not verify leaves
# the one already on the card alone, so pressing the key again costs the time
# and nothing else -- which matters because this link loses a frame about one
# run in six, and trying again is the answer to that. And afterwards, check the
# core is still active before trusting the session: the heartbeat is the
# instrument, and it advances only while the program is presenting frames.
#
# The calculator powers itself down after a few idle minutes, and that state
# looks exactly like a dead keyboard -- the picture stops and no key does
# anything, End included at first. End is the calculator's ON key and wakes it;
# then the arrow keys work again.
#
# Understand what the last line does before running this: ae350run holds the
# Terminal for as long as the program runs, and this program is continuous. The
# window stays busy while the calculator runs, which is normal; F6 switches
# windows, and a board reset is what ends the session. Nothing is reflashed, so
# power-cycling is safe and puts the card's own core back.

if "$CORE" == ""
    CORE=/root/cores/console138k/tiny89.bin
endif
if "$PLAYER" == ""
    PLAYER=/root/ae350/tiemu_smoke.elf
endif
if "$BIOS" == ""
    BIOS=/root/media/bios/TI89Titanium_OS.89u
endif

echo "tiny89: core   $CORE"
echo "tiny89: player $PLAYER"
echo "tiny89: bios   $BIOS"

if test -f "$CORE"
    echo "tiny89: core image found"
else
    echo "tiny89: no core image at $CORE"
    return 1
endif
if test -f "$PLAYER"
    echo "tiny89: program found"
else
    echo "tiny89: no program at $PLAYER"
    return 1
endif
if test -f "$BIOS"
    echo "tiny89: upgrade found"
else
    echo "tiny89: no TI-89 upgrade at $BIOS"
    return 1
endif

echo "tiny89: programming the FPGA"
tangload "$CORE"
if $? != 0
    echo "tiny89: the core did not load"
    return 1
endif

echo "tiny89: sending the program and the upgrade; the calculator boots on the AE350"
echo "tiny89: the keyboard drives it once the picture is up; reset the board to end it"
ae350run "$PLAYER" "$BIOS"
RESULT=$?
echo "tiny89: the program stopped ($RESULT)"
return $RESULT
